  "name": "Using an Alternative Range for the Polar Angle",
  "problem": "When working with polar coordinates, we primarily use the following domain for the polar angle $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n0\\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nHowever, it's sometimes a good idea to use the domain \n\n$$\n-\\pi \\leq \\theta \\lt \\pi.\n$$\n\nChanging the domain for $\\theta$ turns out to be especially useful when a polar petal spans the first and fourth quadrants. \n\nLet's see an example.",
